Third Session
Policy Questions (five items)

Question: When emperors and kings arise, they stand at the center of Heaven and Earth as lords of the cardinal guides and constant virtues; their connection to the moral nature of the people and the teachings of the age is of the utmost weight. Therefore, in governance nothing takes precedence over clarifying human relationships, and among human relationships none is greater than those toward ruler and parent. Past and present discussions of loyalty and filial piety reach their ultimate pinnacle in Yu Shun 虞舜 and the Duke of Zhou 周公; recorded in commentaries and histories, they can be verified with certainty. As for the Book of Filial Piety (Xiaojing 孝經), commentators hold that it was completed by the hands of the disciples of Zengzi 曾子; in clarifying compliance and teaching filiality, it has been revered as a model for generations. By the Han dynasty, someone composed in imitation the Classic of Loyalty (Zhongjing 忠經) in eighteen chapters, yet the purport within its chapters seems not to serve exclusively as instruction for subjects; one wonders whether it can complement the Book of Filial Piety as outer and inner counterparts? We reflect with awe upon How the Grand Progenitor, Emperor Gao (Taizu Gao huangdi 太祖高皇帝), opened Heaven and established the supreme standard, initiating the cultivation of human moral order and bequeathing plans and models that are most vast and far-reaching. The Accomplished Progenitor, Emperor Wen (Chengzu Wen huangdi 成祖文皇帝), made manifest these illustrious achievements and gave peace to the grand plan, composing the book Method of the Mind in Sagely Learning (Shengxue xinfa 聖學心法), in which he spoke at length and in detail on the way of the subject and the way of the son, …
